AXI4-Lite fundamentals (quick recall)

Memory-mapped
Single beat only
VALID/READY handshake
Independent channels
Slave responds after execution

AXI4-Lite SIGNAL GROUPS (pins you actually use)
WRITE CHANNELS
| Channel            | Signals                            |
| ------------------ | ---------------------------------- |
| Write Address (AW) | `AWADDR, AWPROT, AWVALID, AWREADY` |
| Write Data (W)     | `WDATA, WSTRB, WVALID, WREADY`     |
| Write Response (B) | `BRESP, BVALID, BREADY`            |

READ CHANNELS
| Channel           | Signals                            |
| ----------------- | ---------------------------------- |
| Read Address (AR) | `ARADDR, ARPROT, ARVALID, ARREADY` |
| Read Data (R)     | `RDATA, RRESP, RVALID, RREADY`     |

********************************************************************************
1️⃣ AXI4-Lite WRITE TRANSACTION (PIN-LEVEL FLOW)
🔹 Step 1: Write Address Phase (AW channel)
Driven by AXI master-
AWADDR  = target address
AWPROT  = protection info
AWVALID = 1

Slave response-
AWREADY = 1  (when it accepts address)

📌 Transfer occurs when-
AWVALID && AWREADY

🔹 Step 2: Write Data Phase (W channel)
Driven by AXI master-
WDATA  = data
WSTRB  = byte enables
WVALID = 1

Slave response-
WREADY = 1

📌 Transfer occurs when-
WVALID && WREADY

🔹 Step 3: Write Response Phase (B channel)
Driven by AXI slave (your DUT)-
BRESP  = OKAY or SLVERR
BVALID = 1

Master responds-
BREADY = 1

📌 Transfer completes when-
BVALID && BREADY

**************************************************************************************
2️⃣ AXI4-Lite READ TRANSACTION (PIN-LEVEL FLOW)
🔹 Step 1: Read Address Phase (AR channel)
Driven by AXI master-
ARADDR  = target address
ARPROT  = protection info
ARVALID = 1

Slave response-
ARREADY = 1

📌 Transfer occurs when-
ARVALID && ARREADY

🔹 Step 2: Read Data Phase (R channel)
Driven by AXI slave (your DUT)-
RDATA  = read data
RRESP  = OKAY or SLVERR
RVALID = 1

Master response-
RREADY = 1

📌 Transfer completes when-
RVALID && RREADY

****************************************************************************************

APB TRANSACTIONS (PIN-LEVEL)
APB fundamentals
Single master
One transaction at a time
Phase-based
No VALID/READY
Timing is strict

APB SIGNALS 
| Signal    | Meaning                |
| --------- | ---------------------- |
| `PADDR`   | Address                |
| `PSEL`    | Slave select           |
| `PENABLE` | Access phase indicator |
| `PWRITE`  | Read/Write             |
| `PWDATA`  | Write data             |
| `PRDATA`  | Read data              |
| `PREADY`  | Wait-state control     |
| `PSLVERR` | Error indicator        |

3️⃣ APB WRITE TRANSACTION 
🔹 Phase 1: SETUP PHASE
PSEL    = 1
PENABLE = 0
PADDR   = address
PWRITE  = 1
PWDATA  = data

🔹 Phase 2: ACCESS PHASE
PSEL    = 1
PENABLE = 1
If:
PREADY = 1 → write completes
PREADY = 0 → stay in ACCESS (wait)

🔹 End of transfer
PSEL    = 0
PENABLE = 0

4️⃣ APB READ TRANSACTION 
🔹 Phase 1: SETUP PHASE
PSEL    = 1
PENABLE = 0
PADDR   = address
PWRITE  = 0

🔹 Phase 2: ACCESS PHASE
PSEL    = 1
PENABLE = 1

If PREADY = 1:
PRDATA  = valid
PSLVERR = valid
Transfer completes.

*********************************************************************************************
AXI FSMs
Needed because:
Channels are independent
Must wait for handshakes
Must store partial information
But timing is flexible.

APB FSM
Mandatory because:
Protocol is phase-ordered
Signals must change in strict sequence
FSM states map directly to protocol phases.



































































































































